# Wireworm — Screens, the HUD, and audio

This file defines the six screens the game moves between, what each shows, the
three HUD readouts, and the ten audio cues. The keys the screens are driven by
are in `specs/controls.md`, and the run the `playing` screen carries is in
`specs/progression.md`. Every piece of screen copy below carries the name this
specification gives it.

Every piece of text a screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it at
the logical stage size, `1280 x 720`. The palette, the type, and the layout of
each screen are yours.

## The screens

The game is on exactly one of six screens at a time.

| Screen | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | The main menu, and where the game opens. |
| `howto` | How to play. |
| `playing` | Live play, in the three phases `specs/progression.md` states. |
| `paused` | The pause menu, over the frozen board. |
| `victory` | The run was won. |
| `gameover` | The run was lost. |

### `title`

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `WIREWORM` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `CUT THE CURRENT` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `DESCEND`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's items are stacked one above the next under the title and tagline. The
highlighted item is drawn distinctly from the others, so a player always sees
which item `confirm` would take, and the highlight rests on the first item on
arriving at the title. `confirm` takes the highlighted item:

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `DESCEND` | Opens a new run, as `specs/progression.md` states, and moves to `playing`. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Moves to `howto`. |

`back` does nothing on this screen. The board may show behind the menu, dimmed or
otherwise quieted, if that suits the look you design.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as rules of a system. It
covers:

- the goal, which is to cut every worm segment before one reaches the band;
- that a node the worm is turned by gains charge, and that a fully charged node
  detonates when it is shot and arcs through the charged cluster around it;
- that a critical node the worm reaches sends the worm diving straight down;
- that every segment cut by a bolt leaves a node behind, so the field thickens;
- the three foes and what each does to the field;
- the controls.

The controls it names include the fire key, written as the standalone word
`SPACE`, and the movement keys, written as the standalone words `ARROWS` and
`WASD`.

`back` returns to `title`, with the title's highlight at the first item.

### `playing`

The live board. It shows every node at its own charge, every worm, every foe,
every bolt in flight, the arcs of a live discharge, and the cursor inside its
band, together with the HUD bar above. During the `banner` phase it also shows
the level's banner, naming the level about to be played, over the board.

### `paused`

Reached by `pause` during live play. The board stays visible behind the menu and
is frozen: no worm steps, no foe moves, no bolt travels, no phase timer runs, and
no cue plays, so a paused game is exactly where it was when it was paused.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Menu | `PAUSE_ITEMS` | `RESUME`, `RESTART`, `QUIT TO MENU`, in that order |

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `RESUME` | Returns to `playing` with the board and the run exactly as they were. |
| `RESTART` | Opens a new run, at level `1` with `START_LIVES` lives and a score of `0`, and moves to `playing`. |
| `QUIT TO MENU` | Returns to `title`, with the title's highlight at the first item. |

`back` on this screen does what `RESUME` does.

### `victory` and `gameover`

Both end screens carry the same menu, `ENDING_ITEMS`: `PLAY AGAIN`, then `MENU`.

| Screen | Shows |
| --- | --- |
| `victory` | The final score, that all `TOTAL_LEVELS` (`12`) levels were cleared, and the lives remaining. |
| `gameover` | The final score and the level the run reached. |

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `PLAY AGAIN` | Opens a new run, at level `1` with `START_LIVES` lives and a score of `0`, and moves to `playing`. |
| `MENU` | Returns to `title`, with the title's highlight at the first item. |

## The HUD

The HUD bar carries three readouts, all of them inside the bar's own region as
`specs/board.md` states.

| Readout | Shows |
| --- | --- |
| Score | The running score, as digits. It is the most prominent of the three. |
| Lives | The lives remaining, as a row of icons or as a count. |
| Level | `HUD_LEVEL_LABEL` (`LEVEL`), the current level's digits beside it, and `TOTAL_LEVELS` (`12`) on the bar as well. |

How the level readout is composed around those three parts is yours. Nothing else
is required on the bar, and a further readout of your own is welcome there.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write, synthesizing its sounds with
the Web Audio API rather than loading audio files. Define and play exactly the
ten cues below, under exactly these names, one per event.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus. Define and play exactly the ten cues in
`CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event.
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus: define exactly the ten cues in `CUES`
from the game instance's `initialize` and play them from the world's audio inside
a tick, under exactly these names, one per event.
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fire` | `CUES.fire` | A bolt is fired. |
| `cut` | `CUES.cut` | A bolt destroys a worm segment. |
| `discharge` | `CUES.discharge` | A detonation's chain runs. |
| `critical` | `CUES.critical` | A node reaches charge `3`. |
| `foe` | `CUES.foe` | A foe is destroyed. |
| `life` | `CUES.life` | A life is lost. |
| `level-clear` | `CUES.levelClear` | A level clears. |
| `victory` | `CUES.victory` | The `victory` screen opens. |
| `game-over` | `CUES.gameOver` | The `gameover` screen opens. |
| `menu` | `CUES.menu` | A menu highlight moves. |

Each is a distinct short sound, so the ten events are told apart by ear. Each is
played on the frame its event happens and at most once on that frame; a frame
that raises more than one of them plays each of those once.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Muting is yours as well, and the `mute` action toggles it from any screen. While
sound is muted every cue is silent and the game stays fully playable. Audio does
not start until the player has interacted with the page, and a build whose audio
cannot start still runs and still plays.
{{else}}
Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the engine. The game binds the
`mute` action to the engine's mute bit and toggles it from any screen. While
sound is muted every cue is silent and the game stays fully playable.
{{/if}}

## Out of scope

- Network or online play.
- Pointer, touch, and gamepad input. The game is played from the keyboard.
- Persistence of progress, scores, or settings between sessions. Each session
  starts fresh.
- Power-ups, weapon upgrades, or a shop. The cursor's one weapon is the upward
  bolt.
